VT_TALK("sql/sql-design", {
 "process": "Good database design starts with words, not with SQL. Read how the business describes its work, then the nouns become tables and the verbs become relationships. Take a neighbourhood library. Members borrow copies of books, a book can have several authors, and the library may own several copies of one book. So member, book, author, copy and loan become tables. Each gets a primary key, each relationship becomes a foreign key or a junction table, and every promise the business makes, such as a due date after the loan date, becomes a constraint. A loan is an event, so it gets its own table with dates. Think of an architect who listens to the family before drawing the house.",
 "er": "An entity relationship diagram draws each entity as a box and each relationship as a line between boxes. Most tools use crow's foot notation, where a three pronged end means many, a bar means one, and a small circle means optional. There are three kinds of relationship. One to one, like a person and a passport, uses a foreign key with a unique rule. One to many, like a book and its copies, puts the foreign key on the many side. Many to many, like books and authors, needs a junction table with two foreign keys. In the animation, pick each kind and watch Pip place the keys and run a join. Think of a family tree drawn on a chart before anyone writes the names into a register.",
 "conventions": "A few habits keep a schema easy to live with. Pick one naming style, such as plural table names in snake case, and use it everywhere. Avoid spaces and reserved words in names, so nothing needs quoting. Give each table a surrogate id that never changes, and put a unique rule on the natural key, such as email. Name foreign keys after the table they point to, like customer id. Add created and updated times for debugging. When a column holds one of a few values, use a CHECK list for a short fixed list, or a small lookup table when values change or carry facts of their own. Think of a well labelled kitchen where every jar sits in the same place each day.",
 "case": "Here is the whole library schema, built from the story in the first lesson. Members, authors and books each get a table. Book authors is the junction table for the many to many between books and authors. Copies point at their book, and loans point at a copy and a member, with dates. A loan's returned date stays NULL until the copy comes back, and a CHECK makes sure the due date is after the loan date. Two queries show the design paying off, one finds overdue loans and one counts copies still on the shelf. Each requirement in the story is guaranteed by a line of the schema. Think of a blueprint where every room matches something the family asked for.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder. For a beginner, nouns become tables, verbs become links, and rules live in the table. At college level, draw an ER diagram, map each relationship, normalize to third normal form and add constraints. At masters level, design goes through conceptual, logical and physical models, and subtypes such as student members and staff members can be mapped in three standard ways. At research level, people study how to change a design over years without breaking old data, and how to model for document and distributed databases. Think of a town planner who designs roads that can grow when the town grows."
});
